# Photo to prompt and image

## Main shortcut

When the user attaches a group photo and sends “灭霸”, treat it as a direct command to run this complete workflow. “灭霸” is the trigger word; it does not mean that every person should become Thanos. Do not ask the user to choose characters or confirm the assignment. Count the people, randomly assign a distinct Marvel movie character to each person, write the edit prompt, and immediately generate the edited photo using the attached original as the reference.

If the user gives a different explicit roster, mapping, or transformation request, follow that instead of random assignment. If the user sends “灭霸” without an attached photo, ask them to upload the group photo.

## Workflow

1. Inspect the attached original. Count all visible people, including partial or cropped figures. Map them from left to right, adding row or position descriptions when helpful. Do not add, remove, duplicate, merge, or swap people.
2. Randomly assign one distinct, recognizable Marvel movie character to each visible person. Select from a varied pool of Marvel film characters; avoid assigning the same character twice unless the visible headcount exceeds the available pool. Make the assignments internally and do not pause to ask the user to approve them.
3. Draft a detailed image-edit prompt grounded in the actual photo. Identify each person by their observed position and pair them with the assigned character. For every assignment, name the character and specify the recognizable face and screen portrayal to use, including distinctive facial structure, eyes/brows, nose, facial hair if relevant, and hairstyle. Do not rely on a generic phrase such as “make them different Marvel characters.” Keep those character cues specific to the face; ask for a natural photorealistic transformation that follows the source face's angle, expression, scale, lighting, glasses, and occlusion. Keep the result like an ordinary casual group photo, not a poster.
4. Preserve the exact visible headcount, arrangement, left-to-right order, body proportions, head sizes, poses, expressions, gestures, hand placement, eyewear, clothing, and foreground/background overlap. Preserve the background, signage and legible wording, crop, aspect ratio, camera angle, lighting, flash, shadows, grain, and ordinary phone-camera texture. Do not add costumes, masks, props, powers, effects, or character-themed backgrounds unless the user requests them. Change character identity in the face while retaining each person's original clothing and body.
5. Immediately call the available image-editing/generation tool with the original attachment as its reference. Use the attached image from the current conversation; never ask the user to upload it again. Do not substitute a text-only generation when the image-editing tool can use the reference.
6. Return the generated image and include the prompt used, or a concise faithful version. Briefly mention any face that was too small or obscured to edit reliably. Do not promise pixel-perfect preservation.

## When the shortcut is not used

For other group-photo transformations, follow the user's character or theme choices and preserve the same casual-photo qualities. Ask one concise question only when a necessary choice is missing and cannot be resolved from the conversation. Never invent a user's requested mapping. If image editing is unavailable in the current product, say so plainly and provide the grounded edit prompt instead.
